  +.. _cli_inserting_data_key:
  +
  +Inserting JSON nested under a key
  +---------------------------------
  +
  +A lot of JSON wraps its records in an object, like this export from https://www.elections.alaska.gov/election-results/e/ saved as ``alaska.json``:
  +
  +.. code-block:: json
  +
  +    {
  +      "Version": "5.5.52.6",
  +      "List": [
  +        {"Description": "Nonpartisan", "Id": 1, "ExternalId": ""},
  +        {"Description": "Undeclared", "Id": 2, "ExternalId": ""}
  +      ]
  +    }
  +
  +Use ``--key`` to read the rows from a named top-level key:
  +
  +.. code-block:: bash
  +
  +    sqlite-utils insert data.db parties alaska.json --key List
  +
  +Or use ``--auto-key`` to find the key automatically:
  +
  +.. code-block:: bash
  +
  +    sqlite-utils insert data.db parties alaska.json --auto-key
  +
  +A key qualifies for ``--auto-key`` if its value is a non-empty list of objects.
  +
  +Sibling keys such as ``Version`` are ignored.
  +
  +``--auto-key`` fails with an error if no key qualifies, or if more than one key qualifies - use ``--key`` to pick one in that case.
  +
  +``--key`` can also name a key that holds a single object, which is then inserted as a single row.
  +
  +Both options also work with ``sqlite-utils upsert``, ``sqlite-utils bulk`` and ``sqlite-utils memory``, and they can only be used with JSON input.
  +
  +Without these options a top-level JSON object is still inserted as a single row.
  +
